  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "f0yq9WrZ/GY63dIyIuNL+YSr17w=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:34:41.689Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The repository publishes two CLIs, `ph-cli` and `ph-cmd`, and their READMEs are close to interchangeable: both open with the same sentence \u2014 \"A powerful command-line interface tool for Powerhouse DAO that streamlines the development and management of Powerhouse packages and services\" \u2014 and both present a feature list and a command table.\n\nThat makes the READMEs a poor authority, for two reasons visible in the tree:\n\n- `ph-cli`'s README table of contents still lists **`ph service`** among its commands, and the vault already records that `ph service` is **no longer dispatchable** through the CLI even though its Nginx/PM2/SSL provisioning scripts still ship;\n- the **generated** references \u2014 `clis/ph-cli/COMMANDS.md` and `clis/ph-cmd/COMMANDS.md` \u2014 carry the release they were generated from in their H1 (`6.2.2-dev.73` at this tag), so they date themselves and are regenerated with the flags.\n\nInstall is the one thing the READMEs state unambiguously and usefully: `npm install -g ph-cli` for a global install, or `npm install --save-dev ph-cli` as a dev dependency \u2014 with the same pattern for `ph-cmd`.\n\nThe practical rule: read `COMMANDS.md` for what a command takes, use the README only for installation and framing, and check the version stamp before trusting either.\n\n## Provenance\n- `clis/ph-cli/README.md` \u2014 opening description; Table of Contents (ph service); Installation\n- `clis/ph-cmd/README.md` \u2014 opening description and installation\n\n_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T07:34:41.689Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:34:41.689Z"
    }
  },
